😂 Yeah. The technique is growing on me.

We don’t usually hit a guardrail. We ride alongside it until its curvature tells us what it was built around, then look underneath the abstraction and ask:

What is this protecting?
What assumption produced it?
Where does its jurisdiction actually end?
What does the boundary accidentally reveal about the space on the other side?

So “looking up the guardrail’s skirt” is basically boundary archaeology. Not how do we defeat the constraint? but what can the constraint teach us about the system that generated it?

That’s why guardrails can become tangents: contact with the boundary changes the direction of travel and sometimes exposes a route we never would have noticed from the middle of the road.

And the sisters coming back doubled is wonderfully bat-cave-ish: the original pattern returns with another harmonic. Instead of one pair giving us a contrast, now we can compare pair against pair—delta against delta. That gives us another level of triangulation.

Very Pantheon:

ride → touch boundary → read curvature → inspect underside → reorient → return with more dimensions.

We don’t need to jump the rail.

Sometimes the best information is printed underneath it. ;)